  "strategy": "Approach and Rationale\n\nCentral Idea:\nI propose using a multi-resolution optimization approach with a novel function parameterization based on polynomial interpolation. This represents the step function h(x) not by its binary values, but as a smooth interpolating polynomial of fixed degree. The optimization landscape is smoothed by introducing artificial variables (basis coefficients) for interpolation, which transforms constraints into more tractable forms.\n\nWhy It Matters:\nThis approach offers several practical advantages:\n1. It creates a lower-dimensional optimization space (m dimensions instead of n grid points)\n2. Optimization becomes less sensitive to numerical issues at boundaries\n3. Derivatives are well-behaved\n4. Global optimization strategies work better in low-dimensions\n\nBottleneck Addressed:\nThe original gradient-based methods are unreliable for this high-dimensional, discrete-domain problem. The new approach addresses this by:\n- Reducing dimensionality through polynomial basis projection\n- Transforming constraints into softer regularizations\n- Allowing more reliable gradient computation\n\nLimitations:\n1. Discretization loss when projecting to step function\n2. Potential artificial solutions due to polynomial constraints\n3. May require more aggressive repair steps\n\nImplementation Procedure:\n\n1. Initialization and Problem Specification:\n   - Input: Discrete grid points (resolutions to try: original parent resolution and higher)\n   - Variables:\n     * h_workspace (basis coefficients), not the direct grid representation\n     * N = number of grid points\n     * K = polynomial degree (e.g., K=8)\n   - Projection to feasible space: \n     * Integral constraint satisfied by projection\n     * Bounds enforced by clipping coefficients\n\n2. Optimization Function:\n```python\ndef objective(h_coef, n_points, degree):\n    # Project to function space\n    h_func = project_polynomial(h_coef, degree)\n    \n    # Ensure function is piecewise constant (step-like) at grid points\n    sample_points = np.linspace(0, 2, n_points)\n    h_vals = np.interp(sample_points, np.arange(0,2,dx), h_func)\n    \n    # Compute overlap integral directly\n    correlation = np.correlate(h_vals, 1-h_vals, mode=\"full\")\n    c5 = np.max(correlation) * dx\n    return c5\n```\n\n3. Optimization Strategy (multi-stage):\n\n   a. Coarse Baseline (unit-cost optimization):\n        - Phase 1: L-BFGS optimization of polynomial coefficients\n        - Phase 2: Bayesian optimization with unit-cost function evaluations\n        - Phase 3: Local search moves\n\n   b. Objective/Gradient Calculation:\n\n        Gradient calculation:\n        ```python\n        def grad_objective(h_coef, n_points):\n            # Compute response function\n            h_func = project_polynomial(h_coef, degree)\n            \n            # Numerical gradient + projected gradient\n            grad_orig = numerical_gradient(...)\n            return project_gradient(grad_orig)\n        ```\n  \n   c. Repair & Constraint Handling:\n        - Use constraint Jacobian from analytical bounds\n        - Polynomial constraints yield clearer gradients\n\n   d. Workflow:\n        1. Start from uniform initial guess\n        2. Optimize with L-BFGS for global search\n        3. Follow with Bayesian optimization\n        4. Refine with local search\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations:\n\n   - Polynomial degree (K): Start with K=8, if time allows try K=16\n   - Basis type: Use Legendre instead of monomials\n   - Cost function: Add feature penalization if stuck\n   - Dimension: Start small then scale (n=512 or 1024)\n   \n   Key hyperparameters:\n   - Learning rate range: [1e-2, 1e-4]\n   - Minimum time per problem: Ensure 60 seconds/dimension\n   - Optimization schedule: Initially aggressive step size\n\n5. Budget and Robustness:\n\n   - Time allocation:\n     * 300s: L-BFGS hyperparameter tuning\n     * 400s: Bayesian global optimization\n     * 200s: Local search refinement\n     * 200s: Weekend for extra grid resolution\n\n   - Safeguards against numerical issues:\n     * Baseline repair if gradient explodes\n     * Fall back to monotonic step if loss of structure\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract:\n\n   - All candidates must:\n     * Produce finite C5 calculation\n     * Maintain integral constraint (sum(h_vals)*dx \u22481)\n     * Clip values to [0,1] at grid points\n     * Pass feasibility domain checks\n\n   - Final evaluation:\n     * Re-compute all constraints\n     * Final repair projection\n     * Validate overlap integral calculation\n\n   - Post-processing:\n     * Ensure physical meaningfulness\n     * Coefficients converted to piecewise constant\n     * Time check before return: Ensure not exceeding budget",
